meta {
  name: Delete Product
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

delete {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/REPLACE_WITH_PRODUCT_ID
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  `DELETE /api/v3/products/{identifier}` — delete a product by Mongo ObjectId.
  Confirmed live 2026-09-08 against disposable products on "David's Dev Account";
  re-verified with fresh live tests 2026-09-23.

  ## 204 (not the spec's 200)
  `openapi_pimv3.json` declares `200` only. Live: **204 No Content**. A follow-up
  `GET /products/{id}` after deletion returns `404`. Matches `API V3.md`'s prose
  convention.

  ## SKU-as-identifier still rejected
  `GET /products/WC-TEST-FULL-001` (by SKU on the kept fixture) still returns `422`
  after this session created the product itself — rules out any theory that the
  identifier-flexibility claim from `API V3.md` applies to v3 (see `Get Product
  by ID.bru`).

  ## ✅ RESOLVED 2026-09-23 — the "whole-product-delete" subpath hazard is fixed
  Every `.bru` file across this collection has repeatedly warned that
  `DELETE /products/{identifier}/{path}` ignored `{path}` entirely and deleted
  the **entire product** (originally confirmed 2026-09-08, quirk #31; the
  officially-documented "1:M Unlink" pattern `DELETE /products/{id}/categories/{category_id}`
  was independently confirmed to have the identical hazard, quirk #54). **This is
  no longer reproducible as of 2026-09-23** — Plytix appears to have fixed the
  handler. Re-tested three ways on a fresh disposable product with a real, confirmed
  category link:
  1. `DELETE /products/{id}/categories` (a recognized collection name) →
     `405 Method Not Allowed` — `{"errors":[{"name":"MethodNotAllowed",
     "description":"'delete' is not allowed on the 'categories' collection: no
     single well-defined write target."}]}`. Product confirmed fully intact via
     follow-up `GET` (200, all fields unchanged).
  2. `DELETE /products/{id}/totally_made_up_path_xyz` (an unrecognized field
     name) → `404 Not Found` — `{"errors":[{"name":"NotFoundError",
     "description":"Product has no field 'totally_made_up_path_xyz'"}]}`.
  3. `DELETE /products/{id}/categories/{category_id}` (the "1:M Unlink" 3-segment
     pattern) → `404 Not Found` — `{"errors":[{"name":"NotFoundError",
     "description":"Product has no field 'categories/<id>'"}]}`. Not currently a
     working unlink mechanism, but importantly SAFE — no data loss either way.

  **Implication for docs**: the old warning against ever using
  `DELETE /products/{id}/{subpath}` no longer needs to gate any docs — the
  endpoint now fails safely (405/404) instead of silently destroying data. See
  the updated examples below, and `config/api-testing.config.md`'s quirks
  #31/#54 (both now marked resolved).

  ## ⚠️ SUPERSEDED 2026-10-08 — scoped unlink now EXISTS, and `category_ids` is GONE
  The paragraph above used to end by saying that no pattern provides a working
  scoped unlink and that `PATCH` with a reduced `category_ids` array was the only
  way to remove a single category. **Both halves of that are now wrong** —
  re-tested live 2026-10-08:

  1. `DELETE /products/{id}/categories/{category_id}` is now a **working, safe,
     scoped unlink**: `204`, product fully intact, only the named category
     removed. See `Unlink Category from Product.bru` for the captured examples.
  2. `category_ids` on `PATCH /products/{id}` (and on `POST /products`) is now
     **rejected** with `422 "category_ids: Extra inputs are not permitted"`, along
     with `static_list_ids`, `channel_ids`, `ecatalog_ids` and
     `product_data_sheet_ids` — quirk #93. Linking is done with `POST
     /products/{id}/categories` and body `{"id": "<category_id>"}`; see
     `Link Category to Product.bru`.

  The 405/404 examples below remain accurate for the *bare collection* and
  *unrecognized field* forms. Only the 3-segment `categories/{id}` form changed
  behavior, from `404` to a working `204` unlink.
}

example {
  name: 204 - Standard Delete
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-23 against a fresh disposable product WC-TEST-DELETE-CONFIRM-404 (id 6ab41b6debb26b08b2285ef2, created and deleted in the same test run). 204 No Content with an empty body -- correct per HTTP semantics, there is nothing to show beyond the status code. The follow-up GET on this same id, confirming the deletion with a real captured 404, is in "Get Product by ID.bru"'s "404 - Not Found After Delete" example -- see that file for the paired confirmation, matching the convention used by every other resource folder in this collection (e.g. "Delete Asset - Success" / "Get Asset by ID - After Delete").

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6ab41b6debb26b08b2285ef2
    method: delete
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 204
      text: No Content
    }
    headers: {}
    body: {
      type: text
      content: ''
    }
  }
}

example {
  name: 405 - Subpath DELETE Rejected (Hazard Fixed)
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-23 against a fresh disposable product with a REAL, confirmed category link (verified via GET /products?_fields=categories&sku=... beforehand -- the dedicated /categories subpath itself is separately confirmed broken/always-empty, see "Get Product Subpath - Attributes.bru", so it cannot be used to verify linkage). This SUPERSEDES a 2026-09-08 finding that this exact call deleted the entire product (204) -- that hazard is no longer reproducible as of 2026-09-23. The endpoint now correctly rejects the request instead of destroying data; a follow-up GET confirmed the product (and its category link) fully intact. An unrecognized subpath name (e.g. /totally_made_up_path_xyz) returns 404 "Product has no field '...'" instead -- also safe, no deletion. Neither path provides a working scoped-unlink mechanism; use PATCH with a reduced category_ids array to remove a single category instead.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/REPLACE_WITH_PRODUCT_ID/categories
    method: delete
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 405
      text: Method Not Allowed
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": [],
          "errors": [
            {
              "name": "MethodNotAllowed",
              "description": "'delete' is not allowed on the 'categories' collection: no single well-defined write target."
            }
          ]
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}

example {
  name: 404 - 1:M Unlink Pattern Rejected (Hazard Fixed)
  description: Confirmed live 2026-09-23. The officially-documented "1:M Unlink" pattern (DELETE /products/{id}/categories/{category_id}, a 3-segment path naming a specific linked category) is tested here against a real, confirmed category link. This SUPERSEDES a 2026-09-08 finding (quirk #54) that this exact call also deleted the entire product -- no longer reproducible. The 3-segment path is now treated as a single unrecognized field name and rejected with a clean 404, not executed as a delete. Product and its category link both confirmed fully intact via a follow-up GET. This pattern is not currently a working scoped-unlink mechanism (it 404s rather than actually removing just the one category), but it is now safe -- no data loss.

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/REPLACE_WITH_PRODUCT_ID/categories/REPLACE_WITH_CATEGORY_ID
    method: delete
    mode: none
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Accept: application/json
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 404
      text: Not Found
    }
    headers: {
      content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
    }
    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": [],
          "errors": [
            {
              "name": "NotFoundError",
              "description": "Product has no field 'categories/6a9f1b482b6fd055ffb7c7dc'"
            }
          ]
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
